WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.090
DERTOUZOS: Ladies and
gentlemen, welcome.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:20.100
We're looking forward to
informative and fun discussion.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.190
Bill and I will look at the
future, the road ahead--

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:29.600
or what will be, depending
on what book title you like.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.790
We have agreed to focus
on the bigger picture,

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.390
further out, longer range
rather than shorter range,

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.720
stay away from brand name
discussions and issues,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.880
try to see how it might
affect our way of living,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.230
our lifestyle, our society.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.650
Bill will begin.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.210
We'll go for 10 to 15 minutes,
maybe 12 and 1/2, plus or minus

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.730
2 and 1/2.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.640
And I will do the same.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.320
Then maybe we'll fight with
each other for five minutes

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.990
and turn it over
for Q&amp;A live, Q&amp;A

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.732
with microphones, to the floor.

00:01:01.732 --> 00:01:02.440
So let us begin--

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.165
Bill.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:08.860
GATES: Well, it's a
very, very exciting time,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:14.530
in terms of giving people
better tools of communication.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.050
Actually, calling them
tools of communication

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:22.390
may not give the full import of
what a variety of things people

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.520
will be able to do with PCs
connected up to the internet.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.470
I've created a new term,
which is that people either

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:36.605
live a web lifestyle or they
don't live a web lifestyle.

00:01:36.605 --> 00:01:39.550
And what I mean by
that is that somebody

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:45.370
who several times a day goes
to the web, several times a day

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.440
checks electronic mail,
sends electronic mail--

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.550
and for any new topic or
anything they want to do,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.580
they take it as a
given that they're

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.250
going to turn to the
internet to be informed

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.480
and to collaborate--

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.050
that's somebody living
a web lifestyle.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.080
And very few people
are doing that today.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.190
If you want to find large
groups that meet that test,

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.430
you have to go to universities,
particularly some university

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.760
campuses in the
United States, where

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.210
the only way you sign up for
courses is to get on the web.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.400
The way you arrange
things with your friends,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.380
the way you order pizzas,
is through the web.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.240
And my view is that, as
those young people move

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.140
into the economy, and
more and more people

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:41.020
have that kind of experience,
that those consumers will

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:48.430
demand companies to use the web
in a very, very creative way,

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:53.410
whether it's for paying bills
or taxes or selling products

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.350
or simply just informing them.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.140
I think we can already
see that the efficiencies

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.340
to be gained through the new
medium are pretty incredible.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.120
One distinction you can
make is that some people

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.550
will live a web
lifestyle in their office

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.370
well before they do it as
part of their home life.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.370
There's quite a divergence in
terms of the bandwidth that

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.060
will be available to
businesses in the near term

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.060
and in the office,
versus in the home.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.940
If we take, though,
a 10-year period,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:29.560
I think the internet has
been greatly underestimated.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.420
In the next two
or three years, I

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.400
think it's almost
been overestimated.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.050
Some of the articles
that go on and on

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:36.880
about how everybody's
going to be

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.370
shopping there or banking there
or buying their cars there--

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.520
it's just not realistic.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.770
It takes time for
this to happen.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:49.610
Some of the key breakthroughs
have not been made yet.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:53.380
Flat panel screens with
wonderful resolution

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.070
are not yet available.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.550
Now, there are lots
of companies pursuing

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.880
quite a variety of
techniques, including

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.720
straightforward refinement of
approaches that we use today,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.090
as well as revolutionary
approaches,

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:09.910
that I feel certain will
deliver that sometime

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.040
in the next 5 to 10 years.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.410
There are many
software companies

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.480
working on the
very tough problems

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.950
of letting you interface
with the computer in some way

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.270
other than using a keyboard
and a pointing device.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.690
The Microsoft Research
group is very optimistic

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:34.690
that speech recognition, speech
synthesis, visual recognition,

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.430
will be primary ways of
working with a computer.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:41.860
And the cost of the
hardware to do these things

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:42.860
is very straightforward.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.234
You need a pretty
good microphone

00:04:44.234 --> 00:04:46.810
and a semi-decent camera.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:51.790
But the extra cost of those
built into any type of device

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:56.210
will be on the order
of $100 or well

00:04:56.210 --> 00:05:00.100
less as the volume
goes up there.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.750
And so when we talk about the
PC and where the PC is going,

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:11.050
it's important to keep in
mind the rapid innovation,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.010
particularly the rapid
innovation in the user

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.450
interface.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.180
A year ago, people
were talking about

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.300
that the internet and the PC
didn't really go together.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.060
Well, today, through
our efforts and efforts

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.890
of many other
companies, those things

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.790
go together very, very well.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.440
In fact, if you buy a
new PC, the internet

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.530
is a few clicks away.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:35.700
You just merely pick the
service provider that you want

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.930
and how you want to
be billed for that,

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.930
and you're up and running.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.840
Even the software
that lets you do

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.130
internet telephony, internet
meetings, all of those things

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.930
are coming built
into the computer,

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:54.030
so you don't have to go out
and take a lot of trouble

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.980
to set those up.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.265
Right now, people are worried
about the complexity of the PC.

00:05:59.265 --> 00:06:01.260
And this is a very
legitimate worry,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.830
whether it comes in terms
of cost of ownership

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.510
or the difficulty
of updating software

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.840
or the confusion people have.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.670
Once again, I have a very
optimistic viewpoint here,

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.340
partly through inside knowledge
of things that are being done

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.680
and partly just the view of how
rapidly software can improve.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.490
People are going to be
surprised probably in the next

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.770
year, but certainly over
the next several years,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:32.370
on how that PC interface
can be improved,

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.820
how we can take things and
hide them from the user,

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.120
and be doing more for the user,
so that underneath, there's

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:40.630
a lot going on.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:42.570
But when you sit
down to use it, it's

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.051
very, very straightforward.

00:06:44.051 --> 00:06:46.050
You just think of the
documents you want to use.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.360
You think of the topics
you're interested in.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.625
And that kind of experience can
be delivered against the PC.

00:06:53.625 --> 00:06:56.210
It's a very
competitive business.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.300
It's a business where no
product that's out today

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.140
will be popular in
three or four years.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.500
So it's simply a question
of the companies who

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.290
make products-- are they going
to replace their own product,

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.500
or are they going to let
somebody else come in

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:11.700
and do that?

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.940
I thought the picture
that Andy painted today

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.970
was a very important picture.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:21.090
Another speech of his that
I think was quite good

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.220
was one that he gave
at COMDEX, where

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.670
he looked 10 years out
at what kind of chips

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.560
Intel would be making.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.070
And when he talked about
gigahertz, clock speeds,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:37.990
and the instruction speeds being
100 times what they are today,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.760
it's very clear that we
need to do more than just

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.680
run a spreadsheet
or a word processor

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.820
to take good advantage of that.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:50.250
And that's where supply
and demand come together

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.520
and the software that
learns about the user

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.640
and becomes a reasonable
assistant in helping you

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.420
in every way, in this
information economy,

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:01.820
comes across.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:06.690
A final concept is one that
I talk about in my book,

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:10.140
and that's friction-free
capitalism.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:15.000
These tools are going to take
capitalism, which has always

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.930
had the frictional problem
of buyers and sellers

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:24.120
locating each other, it's
going to take that and reduce

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.090
the frictional costs
very, very dramatically.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.610
And so even markets
in consulting,

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.470
which are very poorly
mediated today--

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.659
it's hard to find
the best consultant.

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:35.669
When you find a consultant
you don't know--

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.970
that you didn't look at all the
people who are willing to do

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.580
that work-- it's just that a
friend mentioned it to you--

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.860
markets like that-- the
availability of people,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.110
their references, their price--

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:46.890
all will be there.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.790
And your ability to collaborate
with them at a distance

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:52.170
will be very dramatic.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.720
So it's a good thing
capitalism works,

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.480
because this tool will
be used to bring it

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.710
to new levels of effectiveness
on a global basis

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:06.795
and really make the world
a far smaller place.

00:09:06.795 --> 00:09:08.580
Okay?

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:12.900
DERTOUZOS: This is a lot shorter
than 12 and 1/2 minutes, Bill.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.100
Well, let's see.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.360
I believe we're
headed to about half a

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.210
billion to one
billion interconnected

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.960
computers by the year 2007.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.130
Andy thinks my half a
billion estimate is low.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.380
Now, what will all these
computers, the interconnected

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.860
computers, and the people
behind them be doing?

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.720
I have a simple model
I like to think of.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.670
It's called the
information marketplace,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.940
and it says that all these
computers and their people

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.060
will be doing three things,
one of three things,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.020
or more of them.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:52.830
They'll be buying, selling, or
freely exchanging information

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.510
and information work.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.690
Now, to me, the latter
is very important,

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.610
because when we
think of information,

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:04.580
we think usually of text,
pictures, video, sounds--

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.040
what I like to call
information as a noun.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.240
But there is this other thing,
which is information work--

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.250
massaging information,
altering it,

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.260
which is information as a verb.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:19.100
It's done by computer
programs, like Bill's Microsoft

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.150
Word and others, and it's done
by people, like accountants

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.420
and tax clerks, and so on.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:31.700
It turns out that the fraction
of the industrial economy that

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.340
consists of office
workers is huge.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.540
It's about half of the economy.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.990
So I think a lot
of this new medium,

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:41.270
this new world, the
network society,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:45.800
will really have information
work flowing over it, as well

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:47.900
as, of course, information
as a noun, which

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.960
is the one we talk about, and
the content, and so forth.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.640
But this huge amount,
which is $9 trillion

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.980
of information work,
gives us an idea

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:01.130
of how big things could
be on the financial side.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:04.520
But there will be also free
exchanges and things that will

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:07.310
be not measured in dollars.

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:13.160
One of the things I discuss in
what will be is the possibility

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:17.900
of a virtual Peace Corps, a
gigantic clearinghouse where

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:24.720
the providers of help and those
who wish to receive help find

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.750
their place over
the World Wide Web--

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.280
it's going to be a big
site there, a big server--

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.650
but with a lot of compartments
for different kinds

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:32.830
of activities.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:34.660
There is a lot of
this in the world.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.160
There are a lot of people who
need help, a lot of people who

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.720
can provide it, and why not
provide a clearinghouse where

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.250
these two could be matched?

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.920
And the help doesn't have to
go all ways from the wealthy

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:45.750
to the not wealthy.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:50.400
I could envision a Sri Lankan
doctor providing medical help

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.350
through the network society
to a homeless person in San

00:11:55.350 --> 00:12:00.230
Francisco, where the
care would be affordable.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:03.380
Now, I think the movement
that we're seeing

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:05.630
will be fully integrated
into our lives.

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:08.150
It will be pretty much
like air, water, breathing.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.480
It will not be a cyber-space
out there that we go visit.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:14.310
It will be in everything we do.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:15.300
I agree with Bill.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.870
I think speech will be
the dominant interface,

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:21.510
because the technology
is here and because we're

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.050
born with mouths and ears,
rather than with keyboard

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:25.800
and mouse sockets.

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:32.190
In terms of what
are the big forces--

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.080
can we reduce all
these thousands

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.190
of things we've been
talking about here

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:38.520
to a few fundamental forces?

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:39.390
I believe we can.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:44.990
I believe the first
course is, what

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:48.220
I like to call,
electronic bulldozers--

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:53.120
the ability of our computers
to offload human work

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.860
from the human brain.

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:58.040
I'm not talking about
creativity or complex things,

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:01.160
but simple work that we do
repetitive work, processing,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:04.110
and X-ray annotating
it, et cetera.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:07.030
Offloading human work-- now,
we think that we're doing this,

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:08.460
and we are to some extent.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:10.590
But if you look at what we
do with a World Wide Web

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:14.440
and with electronic
mail, we are using 100%

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.020
our eyes and our brains.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:21.970
In pre-industrial terms, it's
as if we had shovels shoveling.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.330
Then somebody handed us a shovel
that said high-tech shovel,

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:27.120
and we kept shoveling.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:31.090
It's time to stop doing things
with our brains entirely--

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:32.600
clerical, simple things--

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.630
throw the high-tech
shovels away,

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:38.350
and automate a lot of the
activities among the machines

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:40.570
to relieve us of work.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:43.090
I know that computers are
good for a lot of other things

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:46.170
beyond increasing
our productivity.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.950
But no socioeconomic
movement worth its salt

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:52.830
has lasted if it didn't also
help human productivity.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.990
And I think this
one will, and it

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:58.140
might take us even as high
up the productivity curve

00:13:58.140 --> 00:13:59.730
as the industrial movement did.

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:04.470
It may be that we'll see a 300%
increase in office productivity

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.570
during the 21st century.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:10.110
The second force is the force
of electronic proximity,

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:11.610
as I like to call it.

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.050
That is the very
exciting part of the web

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.960
and of these other
technologies that

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.360
brings people closer together.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:21.390
Now, it's amazing how
big this force is.

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:23.650
When we were in
the villages, maybe

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:27.150
we could reach 200 people,
100 people, in our lifetime

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.850
by walking.

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:33.350
When the car came, that number
increased by a factor of 1,000.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:37.209
We could now reach potentially
200,000 people maybe.

00:14:37.209 --> 00:14:38.750
We wouldn't reach
all of them, but we

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:41.050
could reach them by driving.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:44.830
Now, the amazing thing to me
is that the information age

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:49.000
increases this ability by
yet another factor of 1,000,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.300
and we can reach 200,000,000,
300,000,000 people.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:55.730
We won't reach them, but we can.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:57.870
It's a very strange notion.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:00.450
It brings all of
us closer together.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.730
And this world will
have its good things,

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:05.820
like communities of seniors
and handicapped people,

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.320
and all the network
groups that will

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.240
be helping each other,
commercially and otherwise.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.770
But it will also have info
crime and info predators

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.960
and all the problems
that come with proximity.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:19.590
Let me touch a little
bit on culture.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:22.440
There is a great fear that this
increased proximity is going

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.870
to impose a universal
culture on the world

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.030
or that some nation
will take its culture

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:29.160
and try to impose it on another.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:30.490
I won't mention any names.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:37.570
Let me suggest to you that this
new technology that we're all

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:42.370
witnessing has the very strange
capability of strengthening

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:47.000
simultaneously
ethnicity and diversity.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.770
Let me explain.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:53.370
I'm a Greek, and half of the
Greeks are outside Greece.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.800
Half of the Jews
are outside Israel,

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.110
and half of the Palestinians--
and I could go on.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:02.130
Even 20% of the Canadians
are outside Canada.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:04.170
And as the world becomes
increasingly global,

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:06.690
more people will be
outside their landmass

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.490
that is their nation.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:13.770
Now, if we could use the network
society to keep connected

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:17.250
among us the Greeks
outside and inside,

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.880
then we might have a
very strange situation

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:24.340
where, 30 years from
now, 50 years from now,

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:28.800
the Greek nation isn't the
land mass in the Mediterranean,

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.772
but it's a network.

00:16:30.772 --> 00:16:33.970
It's a frightening notion,
the Greek nation network.

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:36.970
Actually, it's much
closer, much closer,

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:38.740
to the notion of nation--

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.290
ethnos, the Greek
notion which implied

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:45.205
that you could be anywhere and
being in that national circle--

00:16:45.205 --> 00:16:47.470
a fascinating thought.

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.090
On the side of
diversity, let's just

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:50.920
look at the European Union.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:52.540
The presence of a
common language,

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:55.760
like English, has
not obliterated

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.270
the national differences.

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:00.730
It's a thin cultural
veneer that permits

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:02.480
the people to communicate
with each other,

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.640
along with other conventions--
taxation, and so on.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:09.710
But underneath are the gigantic,
national, indigenous cultures

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:10.250
of Europe.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:11.166
The Greeks are Greeks.

00:17:11.166 --> 00:17:13.310
Italians are
Italians, and so on.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:18.230
I expect exactly the same thing
to happen in this new medium--

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:23.810
no universal culture, but a
thin veneer, a thin veneer

00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:26.990
of shared culture, that's
going to bind us and level some

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.520
of our differences, help
us understand each other,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.640
but still strengthen ethnicity.

00:17:35.951 --> 00:17:39.250
Wealth-- I believe--

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.050
and here, we
disagree with Bill--

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:43.030
that, left to its own
devices, the movement

00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:46.020
will increase the gap
between rich and poor people

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.110
and between rich
and poor nations.

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:51.580
The reason, to my thinking,
is that information

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:55.060
helps the rich, because it
helps with the material goods

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:58.640
that the rich have and the
services that they offer.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:00.460
So it helps them do
them more efficiently,

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:02.620
and they get better and richer.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:06.190
The poor cannot afford these
technologies or to learn about

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:08.557
them, so they stay behind.

00:18:08.557 --> 00:18:09.640
And the gap will increase.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.590
Mind you, I'm saying,
left to its own devices--

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.620
that means we should help.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:16.940
We should help with a
variety of measures.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:20.760
But if we don't, I believe
the gap will increase.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:26.830
The next point is that, if
we have a billion computers--

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:30.260
which sounds reasonable
in 10 to 15 years--

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:32.590
and on each of them,
there is between 1,000

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:34.690
and a million pieces
of information,

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:37.570
the grand total, if I
do my arithmetic right,

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.640
is between a trillion
and a quadrillion

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.560
pieces of information.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:44.710
And what is useful to this
person is not useful to me.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:47.230
Or what's useful to me is
not useful to that person.

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:51.870
So there's a huge amount of
info junk floating around.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:57.440
And I think that is going
to make necessary middleman

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:01.830
brokers, people and, to
some extent, computers,

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.600
that will help match
the various interests.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:06.210
It's as if, in a
marketplace, people

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.640
came from both sides,
huge numbers of people,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.160
there would be a tremendous
chaos, if there wasn't a way

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.960
to sort things out.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:17.120
I think this is another area
where we might disagree, Bill--

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.880
agents, computer agents,
are overrated and overhyped,

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:23.040
at this stage of
their history anyway,

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.890
and because they can't have
human intelligence or anywhere

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:26.760
near it.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:30.160
So they can't really help much.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:31.720
I think I've talked too much.

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.820
I'd like to say something
about human relations

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.100
and how they fare
through this medium,

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.170
but I'll save this
for a little later on.

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.450
For the time being,
let me just conclude

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:44.860
by saying that I think we
are into this third major

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:47.110
socioeconomic movement,
and we need not

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:49.570
fear it any more
or any less than we

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.840
feared the automobile,
electricity, chemicals,

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:53.890
steam engines.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:55.759
I think we ought to embrace it.

00:19:55.759 --> 00:19:57.550
We are, after all, the
same ancient humans.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:01.030
We've always been with the same
ancient goals and aspirations.

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:03.860
All that's changing
is a new set of tools.

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:05.004
Thank you.

00:20:05.004 --> 00:20:07.926
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:16.810
I think it's between you
and me for five minutes.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:21.770
GATES: Okay, I want to talk
about rich and poor a little

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:25.040
bit and see where the core
of the disagreement is.

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:28.670
I disagree pretty strongly
about rich nation,

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:29.630
versus poor nation.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:36.850
Today if you wanted to know what
somebody's yearly income was,

00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:40.040
and you could only ask them
one question about themselves,

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:43.700
the question you would ask is,
what country do you live in?

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:51.080
That is the dominant factor
for vast differences in income.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:55.010
I claim that, with this
network, the question

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:58.070
you will ask 10
years from now is,

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.160
what is your education level?

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:04.700
And that you'll have a much
better predictor asking

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:09.070
about education level than you
will about their geography.

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:11.090
Now, what do I mean concretely?

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:18.090
I mean that the high-paying jobs
today are service-related jobs.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:22.490
Manufacturing jobs were allowed
to move around the world,

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:26.290
to the person who was willing to
bid for them at the best price,

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:29.320
by low cost transportation.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:35.320
This network now allows that
same geographic dispersion

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.660
of the high-paying jobs.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.040
You can design things.

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:41.140
You can translate things.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:45.970
You can do all those nice
service-oriented things.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:49.050
You can even be a
lawyer and in laws

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:51.790
of another country, if people
are willing to pay you enough.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:56.180
And you can deliver that in
a nice video conference way

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:57.940
and collaborate.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:03.490
And so countries that don't
have good educational structures

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:05.740
will have a problem with this.

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:12.050
But the human resources
of developing countries,

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:14.980
of India and China,
there basically

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.320
is a pretty reasonable
educational system there.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:20.620
And the motivation to become
well-educated in these areas

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:23.080
will be so dramatic,
that I think

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:24.730
we'll actually see a leveling.

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:28.530
And it won't take that long
before we'll see this leveling.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.580
DERTOUZOS: Okay, I fully agree.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.970
I fully agree about
the potential,

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:37.750
and I fully agree even that
we can use that technology

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:40.630
to help in various
parts of the world

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.554
with health, with learning,
with matching labor

00:22:43.554 --> 00:22:44.720
to other parts of the world.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:46.000
I fully agree with all that.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.450
My problem is that, left
to its own devices--

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:52.540
I just did a calculation
and checked it--

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:57.040
in the US, we spend 10% of our
economy on computer hardware,

00:22:57.040 --> 00:23:00.670
computer software, and
the salaries of the people

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.330
who write computer programs.

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:05.740
So you could say computing
is about 10% of our GNP.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:09.280
Germany and Japan
are pretty close.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:12.572
I checked that
number in Bangladesh,

00:23:12.572 --> 00:23:14.530
and we had a lot of
trouble getting the number.

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:16.821
We had to go all the way up
to the Bangladeshi embassy.

00:23:16.821 --> 00:23:19.000
Finally, we estimated it.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:22.090
It was less than
1/10 of 1% today.

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:23.270
Now, that's to be expected.

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:28.000
But Bill, where will the poor
nations and the poor people

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:32.800
within any nation find the
money to buy the hardware,

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:38.200
to buy the software, to get
educated about the indigenous

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:39.640
things that
computers need to do,

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:43.520
so that they can give of
their education and services?

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:47.470
GATES: Well, I guess it
shows my faith in capitalism.

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.680
There will be middlemen
that will emerge

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:56.450
who will invest in the education
and equipping with hardware

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:59.560
people in Bangladesh in
order that they can take

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.220
a 10% fee on the
wonderful service work

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:04.810
that those people will to.

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:08.590
The power of capitalism
to mediate that, I think,

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:09.670
is pretty incredible.

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:13.480
Now, I have to admit
governments have a big role here

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:16.187
in terms of putting in the
communications infrastructure.

00:24:16.187 --> 00:24:18.145
In terms of the large
cities in these countries

00:24:18.145 --> 00:24:21.130
and the universities
there, I think

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:22.600
we're probably okay on that.

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:26.170
Going out beyond that,
I'm partly very optimistic

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:30.340
about satellite systems,
including the Teledesic one

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:32.860
or others like that,
coming along and providing

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:36.280
very high speed bandwidth
to every part of the globe.

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:38.440
And I'm quite
optimistic about, even

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:42.820
as PCs become more powerful and
simpler, that the average price

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:43.580
will go down.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:47.360
So the act of mediation
required here, I don't think,

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:50.860
is very difficult. The one
and only one question mark

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:53.860
on my thing is, is the
education systems--

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:56.946
will they rise to
that challenge?

00:24:56.946 --> 00:24:58.570
Another factor I
think comes into this,

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:02.650
is rich countries a
little bit get lazy.

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:05.200
And the poor
countries try harder.

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:07.540
Given a chance, they
try harder, and you

00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:09.124
get this leveling effect.

00:25:09.124 --> 00:25:10.540
DERTOUZOS: So our
difference seems

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:13.600
to be that you believe that,
through capitalism, middlemen

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:14.440
will help.

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:16.180
And I seem to think
that we need to help

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:19.060
a little more through perhaps
our governments setting up

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:22.240
some libraries, some places
where people can use and learn

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:25.540
about the machines, and through
individual and corporate

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:28.630
donations of help and
money and hardware.

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.360
GATES: No, that's not the
point of disagreement.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:34.360
I think it's important that not
be the point of disagreement.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:38.410
I am absolutely a big
believer that anything

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:43.360
we can do to make this
come true sooner is great.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:47.116
My claim is that year by
year, the gap gets less.

00:25:47.116 --> 00:25:48.490
But I think it's
absolutely worth

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:51.880
having governments and
philanthropists accelerate

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:52.420
that.

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:56.140
The disagreement is, do they go
apart and then come together?

00:25:56.140 --> 00:26:00.670
I claim they go together,
and it's in our power

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:02.590
to make that sooner than later.

00:26:02.590 --> 00:26:05.260
For example, in the
same way that books

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.930
have been available to
people to give them a chance

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:12.640
to read and learn, I think
that in those libraries,

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.830
we should have PCs
connected to the internet.

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:17.560
Anybody should be able
to go in and use those.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:23.151
And I want to make sure that
happens sooner than later.

00:26:23.151 --> 00:26:25.150
DERTOUZOS: Let's leave
that to be the last word.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:27.940
And we spent five minutes
fighting-- not too hard.

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:31.100
But maybe we can do some
more fighting later.

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:35.470
Audience, we have taken 30
precious minutes of your life.

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:38.690
Now we'd like to hear from you.

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:42.020
Please, questions.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:43.310
I know at first, were timid.

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:46.100
But after a while, you'll be
competing for the microphone.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:49.922
So give it a try.

00:26:49.922 --> 00:26:52.130
GATES: If you raise your
hand, there are mics around.

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.296
Someone will bring
you a microphone.

00:26:54.296 --> 00:26:56.150
DERTOUZOS: There is a
hand on that quadrant

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:58.029
and another hand
on this quadrant.

00:26:58.029 --> 00:27:00.903
[FOOTSTEPS]

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:13.050
AUDIENCE: Bill,
Mike-- a question--

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.570
how many hours in
your research do you

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:18.540
find people are able or
willing to spend in front

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.120
of a screen on a daily basis?

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.750
And how will that affect
all the distribution

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:28.260
that you plan to
put through the PC?

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:30.510
DERTOUZOS: Don't
use us as examples.

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.780
GATES: Well, the average
American household somehow

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:37.500
sits in front of a
TV 100 hours a week.

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:39.180
Now, somebody who
sits in front of a TV

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:42.550
zero hours a week, I
find it mind-blowing.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:45.760
I mean, it's not a very
high-quality screen,

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:48.540
and it's not interactive.

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:51.930
So people's ability to sit
in front of a video display

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:54.810
seems to be very high.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:28:00.030
And the quality of these screens
will improve dramatically.

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:02.910
I don't read The Wall Street
Journal or The Economist,

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:04.590
long things, off of the screen.

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:06.450
I still read them on paper.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.280
If I want to look for
a specific article

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:12.480
or get it a little bit
faster than I can otherwise,

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.260
then I'll go onto
the electronic.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:20.250
But I admit that the screen, the
field of view and resolution,

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.600
still has some drawbacks.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.730
But that will go away.

00:28:23.730 --> 00:28:25.740
Eventually, your whole
desk will be a screen.

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:28.200
Your walls will be screens.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:29.730
[CHUCKLING]

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:32.430
Seriously-- come to my house.

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:34.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:35.976
DERTOUZOS: You're invited.

00:28:35.976 --> 00:28:37.012
GATES: Absolutely.

00:28:37.012 --> 00:28:38.970
DERTOUZOS: Let me let me
add a little bit here.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.490
From the history of
this business, which

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:45.300
I've been watching for the last
35 years and living it, at MIT,

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:48.270
we would get students that would
sit in front of the terminal.

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:50.282
In fact, the guy who
invented the spreadsheet

00:28:50.282 --> 00:28:51.240
was a student of mine--

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:52.320
Frankston.

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:54.720
He used to just work so
long that he would finally

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:57.750
fall asleep with his
face on the terminal.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:00.400
And we had to lift him, not
because we were compassionate,

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.035
but to free up the terminal.

00:29:02.035 --> 00:29:04.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.460
But even Frankston, and
everybody else-- or perhaps not

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:09.750
Frankston-- but the
people who exhibited

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:13.450
this addictive behavior, after
a while, lower down [INAUDIBLE]..

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:15.720
And my point is
that we human beings

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:17.370
have thousands and
thousands of years

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:19.320
of experience in adapting.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.090
And my answer to
you, sir, is that we

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:23.250
will adapt to whatever
it takes, so we

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:27.520
don't get too tired, too
anxious, and so forth.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:28.510
More questions?

00:29:36.235 --> 00:29:38.360
AUDIENCE: A couple of
[? telegraphic ?] questions--

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:41.060
my name is [INAUDIBLE]--

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.870
regarding the future
of the network

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:49.710
society or the global
information society,

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:52.800
and just rethinking
the way things happened

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.050
during [? this ?]
century regarding

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:58.980
having people who thought
about having cities,

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:04.180
having buildings, having
highways, and at the point

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:09.210
in time they put the blueprint
of the society around them,

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:11.200
and putting what you've
just said, both of you

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:13.650
together, don't
you think we need

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:17.490
to put, at different
levels, government, industry

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:22.170
professionals, the blueprint
of this global information

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:23.400
society?

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:30.120
One; two-- what about
the Teledesic project?

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:32.040
Is it still in the
dreaming phase?

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:34.080
Or there is anything
which is more

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:39.310
than what has been reported
that you can share with us?

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:43.350
GATES: Well, let me start
with the second part.

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:48.410
Teledesic's a very
exciting company,

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:52.580
because their goal is to build
a very high-speed internet

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:53.900
in the sky.

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:57.080
And it's very
complementary to what's

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:00.680
being done by communications
companies around the world,

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:01.949
in terms of running fiber.

00:31:01.949 --> 00:31:03.740
There are parts of the
world where you just

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:04.970
don't have the density--

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:09.470
even the rural United
States, rural Europe--

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.720
you just don't have the
density to ever run out

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:14.632
the fibers to give those
people the capacity.

00:31:14.632 --> 00:31:17.090
And that's where the satellite,
a lower orbiting satellite,

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:21.080
can come in and give you
extremely high capacity

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:24.270
in these areas, and
then transfer that--

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:26.240
if the destination
is into a city,

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:27.770
transfer it down
to a ground station

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:32.850
where it goes through the optic
fiber network, or vice versa.

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.930
And so Teledesic-- I
hope it's not a dream.

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:39.690
They're spending serious
amounts of my money,

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:44.060
and it's a capitalistic
venture, where

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:47.540
Craig McCaw, who is involved
in the [? seller ?] industries,

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:48.870
is the chairman of the company.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:50.420
There are other
people doing things

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:51.586
that are slightly different.

00:31:51.586 --> 00:31:55.040
So it's not the only company
going after data connections

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:56.180
in the sky.

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:58.010
And I tell you with
great confidence

00:31:58.010 --> 00:32:02.570
that one or multiple of these
schemes will fill in the gap

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:05.870
that the world has
in this connectivity.

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:07.580
The beauty of these
satellite systems

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:10.610
is that the marginal cost
of making it available,

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:14.480
when you're over, say, Mali, or
some territory where you might

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.240
not get much revenue, because
the marginal cost is zero,

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:19.850
you'll find a price,
a clearing price,

00:32:19.850 --> 00:32:24.470
to offer connectivity for
every point on the globe.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:28.430
And so it's a fairly
leveling development

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:34.730
that, with luck, will be in
place within five to six years.

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:37.920
DERTOUZOS: Okay, I don't
want to comment on Teledesic.

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:39.690
But all the comments
that Bill made

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.970
apply to all the other
satellite schemes,

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:43.800
namely the geosynchronous
satellites that

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:47.340
cost a huge amount of
money-- $300, $400, million,

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:48.800
to put up into orbit.

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:51.270
But of course, they
don't have the ability

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:54.522
to go around the world, like
the low-earth orbit ones.

00:32:54.522 --> 00:32:55.980
But all the other
issues-- reaching

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:59.250
a lagoon in the Pacific and
absorbing bursts of traffic

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.630
from the cities-- are there.

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:02.046
I think satellites
are going to be

00:33:02.046 --> 00:33:03.390
a very important part of this.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:06.840
Now, onto your blueprint
question, sir--

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:09.720
I don't know exactly
what you mean by this.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:13.650
The European Union has
already been working on this

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.360
through their various papers.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:17.670
And government in
the US has been

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:20.230
studying the national
information infrastructure.

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:21.780
But I think the
bulk of this drive

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:26.010
is happening by the people,
who are finding ways

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:29.220
to use the internet, and
entrepreneurial companies

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:30.339
are being born.

00:33:30.339 --> 00:33:32.130
And isn't this a little
bit similar to what

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:35.545
happened before, when
manufacturing and the factories

00:33:35.545 --> 00:33:37.920
were set up, and then when
electricity and the automobile

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.440
came, there was a lot of
activity by a lot of people

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:44.580
and, if you wish,
capitalism, which

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:45.930
gave rise to these things.

00:33:45.930 --> 00:33:48.692
So I'm not sure how much
centralization and planning

00:33:48.692 --> 00:33:49.650
should take place here.

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:52.080
Probably it will vary
through the various cultures

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:53.112
of the world.

00:33:53.112 --> 00:33:54.570
GATES: I would say
one thing, which

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:59.760
is I think the goal of
writing a book about this

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:03.780
is to get a broad set of
people to think about what

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:07.860
are the societal issues, to
start thinking about privacy,

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:09.870
and have, versus have-nots.

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.239
And so to the degree
that there needs

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:13.860
to be a broad agreement
on a blueprint

00:34:13.860 --> 00:34:17.250
for those tough issues,
I agree with you.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:19.929
In terms of a blueprint
of what it all looks like,

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:22.949
it's a lot better to just let
marketplace competition play

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:23.610
this thing out.

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.646
The opinions about
what people are

00:34:25.646 --> 00:34:27.270
going to do on the
internet-- you know,

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:29.400
just think two years
ago what they said

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:30.690
would be happening now.

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:35.330
I mean, people were
wrong, 90% wrong.

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:41.730
And I think the way that
companies succeed and fail,

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:45.360
this is a wonderful thing
for high-risk capitalism

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:46.800
to jump in.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:49.320
DERTOUZOS: More questions?

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:53.820
AUDIENCE: Gentlemen, given the
fact that our society today--

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.730
all our social systems
and value systems

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:00.720
are centric around
employment, and given

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:04.900
your ideas about massive
increases of productivity,

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:09.600
what's your vision of that
relationship between technology

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:11.070
and employment?

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:12.630
DERTOUZOS: I
struggled with this.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:13.570
I struggled with this.

00:35:13.570 --> 00:35:14.070
Thank you.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:15.720
It's a very good question.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:18.690
And I even got spanked
several times by my colleague

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:22.310
Bob Solow, the Nobel
Prize winner in economics.

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:24.060
Here is what I know,
and it's very little.

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:30.270
I believe productivity
will increase.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.667
We have no assurances, okay?

00:35:31.667 --> 00:35:33.500
But we believe-- I
think, generally, there's

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:34.940
a belief it will increase.

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:37.730
After all, things are
done inefficiently today.

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.980
And when we apply
all this horsepower

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.560
to things that are done
haphazardly and efficiently,

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:43.850
you expect some improvement.

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:46.040
So that will increase.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:48.950
The problem is that for
employment or unemployment

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:53.240
to move, we have to understand
what happens to demand.

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:56.270
Because unemployment
happens when

00:35:56.270 --> 00:35:58.360
those two are out of kilter.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:00.110
The productivity
increases, and demand

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:02.254
doesn't increase, for example.

00:36:02.254 --> 00:36:03.920
Then people are thrown
off on this side,

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:06.300
and they cannot do work.

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:07.940
Now, there will
be displacements,

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:09.170
even if those two attract.

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:12.590
So I'm convinced that there
will be changes in jobs.

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:14.390
People who are doing
job A now will not

00:36:14.390 --> 00:36:16.130
have that job in
the future, even

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:18.260
if productivity doesn't change.

00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:20.970
But the key issue
behind your question,

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:22.970
what will happen to
employment/unemployment,

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:24.920
hinges on what will
happen to demand.

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:28.130
And all my economist
friends say we don't know.

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:32.710
So I'm afraid the
answer is we don't know.

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:37.550
GATES: I have a little bit
more optimistic view of this.

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:42.620
Essentially, demand in
some areas is finite.

00:36:42.620 --> 00:36:45.770
When farmers got
very, very productive,

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:48.170
and the price of food
came down, people

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:50.130
didn't eat 10 times as much.

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:52.310
I mean, they ate meat,
so they eating more cows.

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:53.510
But there was a limit.

00:36:53.510 --> 00:36:56.520
When radial tires came into the
car market, people then say--

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:59.300
wow, these tires are better;
we'll drive a lot more--

00:36:59.300 --> 00:37:02.780
and so that the amount of
society's resources in the car

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:04.390
market shrank.

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:07.940
If you think of the resources
that go on today to printing

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:10.340
bills and mailing them out
and answering questions

00:37:10.340 --> 00:37:12.500
about those, that's huge.

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:16.640
And this technology will greatly
reduce the number of people

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:18.800
you need to handle that.

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:22.730
But there are areas where
I see near infinite demand.

00:37:22.730 --> 00:37:24.350
Take education.

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:26.570
You know, why not?

00:37:26.570 --> 00:37:29.240
If there's productivity
here, why not

00:37:29.240 --> 00:37:32.610
have five children
in a classroom?

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.060
Take medicine-- if there's
all this productivity,

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:38.600
why not have much better
personalized health care?

00:37:38.600 --> 00:37:40.910
Take leisure time activities--

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:43.850
with all this productivity, we
should have more options there.

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:45.710
We'll have more
wealth to spend there.

00:37:45.710 --> 00:37:50.000
Eventually, you'll even
shorten the workweek.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:53.060
People will choose to
take more time off.

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:55.960
Maybe Americans will
take longer vacations.

00:37:55.960 --> 00:37:57.560
I think that would be nice.

00:37:57.560 --> 00:37:59.080
DERTOUZOS: That's
not in the cards.

00:37:59.080 --> 00:38:00.430
GATES: Not in the near term--

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:02.960
but if you're talking
about a situation

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:06.140
where all these productivity
plays out 20 years from now,

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:08.780
I believe it's very, very
much what will happen.

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:11.810
DERTOUZOS: In principle, elected
leisure is available to us.

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:15.200
The thing that's scary is
that the Industrial Revolution

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:17.640
made it possible for us,
with this multi-fold increase

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:21.457
in productivity, to basically
live in an essential way,

00:38:21.457 --> 00:38:23.540
the way we lived before
the Industrial Revolution,

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:25.190
by working much less.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:28.590
But our working hours kept
up and even increased,

00:38:28.590 --> 00:38:31.910
so there is something inside
us that keeps us working hard.

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:32.800
But I agree with you.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:34.220
Elective leisure is an option.

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:36.620
And maybe in the
long future, it may

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:38.150
be a way in which we will go.

00:38:38.150 --> 00:38:39.770
Let's get some more questions.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:40.930
Yes?

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:43.700
AUDIENCE: A follow-up on
governance point that we are

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:47.330
discussing, the impact of
information sharing on the way

00:38:47.330 --> 00:38:50.120
the societies will be run--

00:38:50.120 --> 00:38:56.295
NBC, MS, CNN, Berlin Wall,
penetration of information

00:38:56.295 --> 00:38:58.580
to the society at large--

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:01.250
what do you think it will do
to the role of the governments,

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:04.750
governments and
governance at large?

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:08.087
DERTOUZOS: Do you want
to take that or not?

00:39:08.087 --> 00:39:10.170
GATES: I think there's
some exciting opportunities

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:12.210
with government.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.792
I think citizens will be
more educated on the issues.

00:39:15.792 --> 00:39:18.000
They'll have a chance not
just to read the headlines,

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:19.350
but to dive in.

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.130
If the headline says,
Budget cut by $20 billion,

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:24.360
they can go in and see,
well, what was the budget?

00:39:24.360 --> 00:39:25.590
How was the money spent?

00:39:25.590 --> 00:39:29.130
How has that changed over time?

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:31.410
There's various debates
about how far we should

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:33.120
go with direct democracy.

00:39:33.120 --> 00:39:36.150
But no longer will the argument
against direct democracy

00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:39.575
be that it's just too
expensive to go out and consult

00:39:39.575 --> 00:39:40.200
all the people.

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:43.200
We'll have a mechanism that
makes that a possibility.

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:46.530
And the economists pointed
out that Switzerland uses

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:49.590
that with reasonable success.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:55.350
And I think we'll see more
and more push along that line.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:59.040
I think government will be the
greatest beneficiary in terms

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:02.370
of its efficiency, the
way it tracks things,

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:04.050
uses paperwork,
gathers knowledge,

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:05.370
shares that knowledge.

00:40:05.370 --> 00:40:06.460
It's ripe.

00:40:06.460 --> 00:40:09.180
Take tax collecting
or medical payments--

00:40:09.180 --> 00:40:12.840
these things can be made
an order of magnitude

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:14.250
more effective.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:16.050
I don't think any
government will

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:19.350
be able to restrict information
flow to its citizens

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:21.000
as much as in the past.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:24.300
Every advance in communication,
from the phone to the fax

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:27.060
machine, have made that
far more difficult,

00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:29.760
and the internet just
takes that to a new level.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:33.000
And so governments will have
to operate in an environment

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:39.045
where citizens have access to
the full range of opinions.

00:40:39.045 --> 00:40:40.920
DERTOUZOS: I fully agree
with what Bill said.

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:44.340
Let me add something else--

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:46.920
big question raised
in various settings

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:50.340
here in Davos about
governments is

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:55.410
what is their role
insofar as the information

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:58.260
crossing national boundaries?

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.230
Whereas, the internet
isn't subjected

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:05.790
to any passports or checkpoint.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:09.330
I have some suggestions
about that for governments.

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:13.110
I think within a
nation, the government

00:41:13.110 --> 00:41:16.740
should try to preserve
the cultural status

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:21.990
quo of that nation by using
technology to that end.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:23.590
Let me explain.

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:27.060
If you believe, in your nation,
that anonymity should never

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:29.670
exist, then you
can use technology

00:41:29.670 --> 00:41:32.190
of public key cryptography,
require every message

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:35.400
to be signed, which will
call for a public key

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:36.450
to be used to read it.

00:41:36.450 --> 00:41:38.250
And then you'll know
who sent that message

00:41:38.250 --> 00:41:40.290
or who was it that signed.

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:43.620
If, on the other hand, you want
to be open, you can do that.

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:45.480
If you want to have
a rating system,

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:48.990
there is technology so that
even third parties can rate

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:50.500
the sites that you can access.

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:54.120
So within a nation,
I think governments

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:57.090
should try to preserve
the cultural status quo.

00:41:57.090 --> 00:41:58.590
Because thousands
of years have been

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:00.881
spent to build up this cultural
status quo, and nothing

00:42:00.881 --> 00:42:01.470
has changed.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:03.490
The people are still the same.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:06.300
But the difficult thing is
what happens between nations?

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:11.670
And there, I think governments
should strike agreement

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:13.290
among each other
internationally.

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:15.660
We've had the same
situation with crime.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:18.030
We've had the same
situation with trade.

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:19.480
And I think we
should do the same.

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:21.210
They should begin
striking agreements

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:23.610
as to what they will
do with each other

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:27.390
if there are violations through
their boundaries and borders.

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:30.010
GATES: Well, I think I
can agree with all that.

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:33.750
But there are a few
very tricky problems.

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:38.760
Let's say somebody in
the US writes something

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:43.410
that is sort of Nazi propaganda,
which is clearly allowed

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:45.890
by the freedom of
speech in the US

00:42:45.890 --> 00:42:49.440
and is clearly banned
by German laws.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:51.730
Who should deal with that?

00:42:51.730 --> 00:42:53.700
Well, the US
government is not, I

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:56.820
can almost assure you,
going to force a rating

00:42:56.820 --> 00:42:59.130
to be put on that page.

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:03.150
In Germany, you have many
internet service providers,

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:06.810
none of whom have the resources
to filter the World Net

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:09.460
and see if there's anything
appropriate out there.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:11.190
So in between the
person who published

00:43:11.190 --> 00:43:14.100
in the US and the person
who's receiving that--

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:17.070
which, say, the Germans
don't want to have happen--

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:21.720
who's going to find
that and block it?

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:24.480
And unfortunately,
country by country, you're

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:26.640
going to have to get
resources together

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:29.970
to see which things
are out there that you

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:31.297
don't want to come in.

00:43:31.297 --> 00:43:33.630
You can have a policy of only
letting things in that you

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:36.627
review, but that would
probably overwhelm you,

00:43:36.627 --> 00:43:38.460
and you'd lose the
benefits of the internet;

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:42.864
or have a policy of just
blocking certain named things.

00:43:42.864 --> 00:43:44.280
But you'll have
to accept the fact

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:45.970
that things will get through.

00:43:45.970 --> 00:43:50.880
And so whatever
culture has relied

00:43:50.880 --> 00:43:56.130
on restricting
information flow, that

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:59.370
won't be there as a tool
as much as it has been.

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.830
DERTOUZOS: Well, I agree that
there will be imperfections.

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:03.704
I don't want to take
more time debating this.

00:44:03.704 --> 00:44:06.220
Let me make a couple
of announcements.

00:44:06.220 --> 00:44:10.500
First of all, following
immediately after this session

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:13.500
will be the president of
South Africa, Mr. Mandela,

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:17.730
who has agreed to come and
speak for a short while.

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:22.790
And the audience is asked to
please stay for his address.

00:44:22.790 --> 00:44:25.200
The second announcement
I'm asked to make

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:29.814
is speakers, please identify
yourselves-- your name

00:44:29.814 --> 00:44:31.980
and where you're from--
before asking your question.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:33.750
Let's move on to a
next question, please.

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:34.980
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

00:44:34.980 --> 00:44:35.952
DERTOUZOS: Yes, ma'am?

00:44:35.952 --> 00:44:38.160
AUDIENCE: Margaret Wilson,
chairman of Scarborough's,

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:39.360
Austin, Texas--

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:40.980
I'm interested in
your perspectives

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:43.350
on future meetings of
the World Economic Forum,

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:45.450
either here in Davos
or around the world,

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:47.760
and how technology
may affect these

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:50.490
and how they may be
different as a result,

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.800
realizing that Klaus
Schwab has already brought

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.140
a great deal of technology
here and provided us

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:58.770
with computers to use for
messages and other services?

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:02.320
And secondly, would
be interested, Bill,

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:05.760
in your sharing a little bit
more with us about your home

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:07.950
as related to technology
and how that may

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:09.965
affect our homes in the future.

00:45:09.965 --> 00:45:11.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:11.330 --> 00:45:17.480
GATES: Okay, well, if
my home is the future,

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:19.610
then there'll be a
lot of large homes

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:22.580
that take a long
time to get built.

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:26.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:26.050 --> 00:45:28.700
The key neat thing I've
done with this home

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:32.840
is really pushed the limit
on screen technology and by--

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:34.820
put a lot of screens
all over the place,

00:45:34.820 --> 00:45:41.030
that it'll be quite some time
before those are mass market

00:45:41.030 --> 00:45:42.490
type items.

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:47.000
There are LCD
projector technologies.

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:51.140
There's a chip technology
called direct digital mirror

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:54.600
device that's a Texas
Instruments approach.

00:45:54.600 --> 00:45:59.480
And these are very neat
ways of displaying images

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:01.580
with incredible quality.

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:05.480
And so there are
many companies that

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:07.910
are gathering image
libraries and putting those

00:46:07.910 --> 00:46:09.254
onto the internet.

00:46:09.254 --> 00:46:10.670
I'm involved with
a company called

00:46:10.670 --> 00:46:14.120
Corvus that does that and does
it at very high resolution.

00:46:14.120 --> 00:46:18.330
And those images are indexed so
that you can find them easily.

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:21.320
So if I'm, one day,
at home, saying, hm,

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:23.870
I'm interested in
Russia, I can have

00:46:23.870 --> 00:46:25.460
images come up on
all the screens

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:27.120
of the house about Russia.

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:28.310
And if I'm passing by one--

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:31.970
I'm curious about its background
and more images like that--

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:34.730
I just click on a
little remote control

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:36.860
and get that information
and see more.

00:46:36.860 --> 00:46:41.840
So whether it's Renaissance
art or sailboats or sunsets,

00:46:41.840 --> 00:46:44.070
any topic, those images come in.

00:46:44.070 --> 00:46:50.300
And it's very fun,
informative, and educational.

00:46:50.300 --> 00:46:51.920
That's probably the
thing that's been

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:54.860
done that's the most unusual.

00:46:54.860 --> 00:46:57.650
DERTOUZOS: Okay, let me take
the other part of your question,

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:01.480
about the World Economic Forum.

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:03.782
To answer that question,
it's a broader question

00:47:03.782 --> 00:47:05.240
that you're raising,
I think, which

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:07.310
is really what
kind of activities

00:47:07.310 --> 00:47:08.960
pass and do not pass?

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:11.180
What kind of human
activities pass and do not

00:47:11.180 --> 00:47:14.550
pass through the wires and the
wireless links of the network

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:15.180
society?

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:17.780
And that's a
fascinating question.

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:20.510
Let me suggest some
pretty simple things--

00:47:20.510 --> 00:47:26.170
straight text information,
images, video,

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:28.460
pass rather nicely.

00:47:28.460 --> 00:47:30.980
Video conferencing-- we could
be talking to each other

00:47:30.980 --> 00:47:33.020
and passing signals
to each other.

00:47:33.020 --> 00:47:37.640
Emotions-- I believe
they passed partially.

00:47:37.640 --> 00:47:39.560
We cry and laugh
in front of our TV.

00:47:39.560 --> 00:47:42.020
I don't see why we won't cry
and laugh in front of this.

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:44.120
So to the extent you
cry and laugh here,

00:47:44.120 --> 00:47:47.180
you can carry that through
the electronic world.

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:49.580
But there are some
things, I submit,

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:52.190
that do not pass at all.

00:47:52.190 --> 00:47:55.646
And maybe I can demonstrate.

00:47:55.646 --> 00:47:56.520
Can I touch you here?

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:56.910
GATES: Sure.

00:47:56.910 --> 00:47:57.610
DERTOUZOS: Good, thank you.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:47:58.110
[CHUCKLING]

00:47:58.110 --> 00:48:00.150
If Bill-- I don't know.

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:01.340
Maybe he would react badly.

00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:03.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:07.280
If Bill had not gone to
Harvard, if he had come to MIT,

00:48:07.280 --> 00:48:08.857
and if you hadn't left Harvard--

00:48:08.857 --> 00:48:10.940
God knows what would have
happened to the software

00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:13.880
industry-- but it would
illustrate my point.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:16.010
If he was graduating
from MIT, and he

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:18.140
was my student,
and a good student,

00:48:18.140 --> 00:48:21.290
and all that, I could grab
him here by the collar,

00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:24.850
and I could say,
Bill, I expect you

00:48:24.850 --> 00:48:29.120
to do very well when
you graduate from here.

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:31.580
Now, I could email
that to him, right?

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:33.230
But it wouldn't be the same.

00:48:33.230 --> 00:48:36.149
I could pass it
through video, and it

00:48:36.149 --> 00:48:37.190
would be a little better.

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:39.050
But still, he wouldn't
feel a pain here.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:42.140
Or I could use the things we
describe in our books, which

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:44.930
are the tactile things you
talk about and the body nets

00:48:44.930 --> 00:48:46.640
and the bodysuits I talk about.

00:48:46.640 --> 00:48:49.910
So either he could wear a
suit and feel the pressure

00:48:49.910 --> 00:48:54.110
while he sees me approaching,
or a robot could grab him.

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:56.180
I mean, any one of these
solutions could work.

00:48:56.180 --> 00:48:57.140
But let me--

00:48:57.140 --> 00:48:58.430
GATES: Or electrical shock.

00:48:58.430 --> 00:48:58.980
DERTOUZOS: Or electrical shock.

00:48:58.980 --> 00:48:59.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:04.212
But let me make the point that
that is not the same thing.

00:49:04.212 --> 00:49:05.420
Now, this is a serious point.

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:06.920
Why?

00:49:06.920 --> 00:49:10.130
Because both Bill
knows and I know

00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:12.440
that we can turn off the
switch and stop the robot.

00:49:12.440 --> 00:49:14.780
So the whole thing is cerebral.

00:49:14.780 --> 00:49:16.670
It's not a primal force.

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:19.430
And I believe these
primal forces of the cave,

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:22.620
the forces that we felt,
when millions of years ago,

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:25.730
we were in the cave-- the
animal growling at the door

00:49:25.730 --> 00:49:29.390
or the nurturing we felt
for a loved one or the food,

00:49:29.390 --> 00:49:32.950
the wish for those things--
those primal forces cannot

00:49:32.950 --> 00:49:33.450
pass.

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:35.825
Now, you're going to say, how
many of these primal forces

00:49:35.825 --> 00:49:37.640
do we use in the
World Economic Forum?

00:49:37.640 --> 00:49:41.270
I suggest that we use a lot
of them in our daily life.

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:42.950
The relationships
between siblings,

00:49:42.950 --> 00:49:45.380
between doctor and
patient, the trust

00:49:45.380 --> 00:49:47.330
that we build with
business associates

00:49:47.330 --> 00:49:49.260
are all based on the
forces of the cave.

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:53.120
And at MIT, where we're thinking
of having virtual classes,

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:55.880
we will be requiring
students to come back

00:49:55.880 --> 00:49:58.730
every year or
every two semesters

00:49:58.730 --> 00:50:00.680
to take courses
on campus, so they

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:03.950
can feel these forces of
the cave, build the trust,

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:05.100
recharge the battery.

00:50:05.100 --> 00:50:07.280
So I think, in answer
to your question,

00:50:07.280 --> 00:50:09.830
a lot can happen
through the network.

00:50:09.830 --> 00:50:12.195
But ultimately, you'll
have to come back to Davos.

00:50:12.195 --> 00:50:14.175
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:14.175 --> 00:50:17.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:19.130 --> 00:50:21.040
GATES: It's a very
good question,

00:50:21.040 --> 00:50:24.970
because, although face-to-face
meetings will always

00:50:24.970 --> 00:50:28.970
be superior in
many, many respects

00:50:28.970 --> 00:50:33.190
to even the best video
conferencing environments,

00:50:33.190 --> 00:50:36.310
sometimes a face-to-face
meeting is so much trouble,

00:50:36.310 --> 00:50:39.080
you'll take the lesser approach.

00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:41.290
Videoconferencing
business-to-business

00:50:41.290 --> 00:50:43.590
is going to catch on wildly.

00:50:43.590 --> 00:50:45.892
I mean, when people see
this welcome system,

00:50:45.892 --> 00:50:47.350
a lot of what
they're responding to

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:49.090
is high-quality
videoconferencing,

00:50:49.090 --> 00:50:53.710
whose price is getting
to be very reasonable.

00:50:53.710 --> 00:51:01.060
And so it's a very good
question, how many things

00:51:01.060 --> 00:51:02.320
like this there will be?

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:04.870
The speeches-- we're all
sitting in a room here--

00:51:04.870 --> 00:51:08.410
that can be recreated through
technology very easily.

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:10.600
What can't be recreated
is when you get up,

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:13.540
and you're mingling around,
meeting somebody new,

00:51:13.540 --> 00:51:17.090
running into somebody at dinner,
and kind of the isolation

00:51:17.090 --> 00:51:19.720
we have, that we're all here
and in this common meeting--

00:51:19.720 --> 00:51:22.647
which is a very
important element of it.

00:51:22.647 --> 00:51:24.980
DERTOUZOS: Okay, I think we've
answered this one enough.

00:51:24.980 --> 00:51:26.792
Let's go to another question.

00:51:26.792 --> 00:51:28.750
Yes, and then on the
other side, please-- first

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:29.884
here and then there.

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:34.400
AUDIENCE: My question is--

00:51:34.400 --> 00:51:37.820
my name is [INAUDIBLE],,
president of Africa Online.

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:42.560
My question would be, given
that the internet will encourage

00:51:42.560 --> 00:51:45.440
forces of capitalism,
isn't there

00:51:45.440 --> 00:51:48.177
going to be a time where we're
going to need regulation?

00:51:48.177 --> 00:51:49.760
And if we're going
to need regulation,

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:51.410
who would be doing it?

00:51:51.410 --> 00:51:53.820
Otherwise, the whole system
will crack at some stage.

00:51:56.224 --> 00:51:57.890
DERTOUZOS: Well, I
think I answered this

00:51:57.890 --> 00:52:01.070
by asking for regulation
first within the countries,

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:03.400
for the activities within
the country, and then

00:52:03.400 --> 00:52:07.040
pairwise regulations, as
we do with trade and crime,

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:08.390
for cross-border activities.

00:52:08.390 --> 00:52:11.440
But Bill, you may want
to add more to that.

00:52:11.440 --> 00:52:15.920
GATES: The question was
about restrictions on content

00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:16.460
primarily?

00:52:16.460 --> 00:52:18.668
DERTOUZOS: Did you mean
restrictions on content, sir?

00:52:18.668 --> 00:52:21.020
AUDIENCE: No, it's
mostly international,

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:22.920
mostly from an international
point of view--

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.840
you're going to get certain
companies getting really big,

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:29.870
to the extent probably where
they can control regulation

00:52:29.870 --> 00:52:31.750
and other governments.

00:52:31.750 --> 00:52:33.620
And when they come
onstage, we probably

00:52:33.620 --> 00:52:37.439
should start regulating it early
enough before it's too late.

00:52:37.439 --> 00:52:38.480
Is there a need for that?

00:52:38.480 --> 00:52:41.994
And if there is, who
would be the right body

00:52:41.994 --> 00:52:43.910
at the international
scale [? of ?] regulation

00:52:43.910 --> 00:52:45.326
at this stage?

00:52:45.326 --> 00:52:46.700
GATES: Well, it's
never too late.

00:52:46.700 --> 00:52:48.158
AUDIENCE: This is
probably targeted

00:52:48.158 --> 00:52:50.157
towards Bill and
Microsoft, because there

00:52:50.157 --> 00:52:52.490
are people who are already
complaining that it's already

00:52:52.490 --> 00:52:53.954
too big.

00:52:53.954 --> 00:52:55.210
GATES: Well, geez.

00:52:55.210 --> 00:52:58.720
Microsoft is actually a pretty
smart company, honestly.

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:02.240
It may be one of the smallest
companies here at this event.

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:08.440
It's certainly not in the
top 1,000 large companies.

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:11.710
Sectors of the economy,
where prices can come down

00:53:11.710 --> 00:53:14.830
by a factor of four, as
they have in software,

00:53:14.830 --> 00:53:17.230
by having such
rapid improvements,

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:22.250
those are the ultra competitive
sectors of the economy.

00:53:22.250 --> 00:53:26.620
And I wish I was in an industry
where somebody's future was

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:28.300
guaranteed.

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:30.430
But if you take any
industry, and you say,

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:32.900
what leader of an
industry might be

00:53:32.900 --> 00:53:36.400
displaced in the next 10 years?

00:53:36.400 --> 00:53:39.220
You would pick the leaders
of the technology business.

00:53:39.220 --> 00:53:42.010
They are the most at risk.

00:53:42.010 --> 00:53:43.960
They are surfing the wave.

00:53:43.960 --> 00:53:46.885
If they miss a technology,
if they don't listen well

00:53:46.885 --> 00:53:49.150
to their customers, if they
don't hire smart people,

00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:50.680
they are gone.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:52.180
Whereas, in many
other businesses,

00:53:52.180 --> 00:53:55.180
you have brand assets,
manufacturing assets,

00:53:55.180 --> 00:53:58.190
distribution assets, that
are great protection.

00:53:58.190 --> 00:54:02.450
And that's why the
technology business people--

00:54:02.450 --> 00:54:04.000
they run scared.

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:07.600
Certainly, we do, in
terms of thinking, boy,

00:54:07.600 --> 00:54:09.530
will we be the one
to do a new product?

00:54:09.530 --> 00:54:13.030
So I think the
toughest parts of this

00:54:13.030 --> 00:54:17.170
relate to the network and
pricing of the network,

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:19.540
because there, it's
more economical to only

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:23.710
have one person build a lot
of very high-speed elements

00:54:23.710 --> 00:54:24.490
in the network.

00:54:24.490 --> 00:54:27.550
And yet, having the right
touch of regulation,

00:54:27.550 --> 00:54:29.200
where you can
encourage innovation

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:32.260
and yet, you know what the
pricing model should be,

00:54:32.260 --> 00:54:34.990
that's still as yet
to be figured out.

00:54:34.990 --> 00:54:38.470
DERTOUZOS: I do not have
this fear of bigness,

00:54:38.470 --> 00:54:42.070
corporate bigness, because the
technology helps the individual

00:54:42.070 --> 00:54:44.530
as much or more than it
helps the large company.

00:54:44.530 --> 00:54:45.730
Let me explain.

00:54:45.730 --> 00:54:47.950
In the industrial
age, you could not

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:52.000
build a car or any other major
product in your living room

00:54:52.000 --> 00:54:53.530
or in your study.

00:54:53.530 --> 00:54:56.470
But in this information
age, you can certainly

00:54:56.470 --> 00:55:00.190
sell your information work
or your information product

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:02.320
or services from your home.

00:55:02.320 --> 00:55:05.830
So I think what this does,
it gives a capability

00:55:05.830 --> 00:55:10.900
to a lot of individual
people to sell

00:55:10.900 --> 00:55:13.240
products, services, information,
as well as to freely

00:55:13.240 --> 00:55:14.350
exchange it.

00:55:14.350 --> 00:55:16.540
And I think it counteracts
some of the fears

00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:19.570
that you have about size.

00:55:19.570 --> 00:55:23.890
The other thing it does-- and
it was raised here in this forum

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:28.250
one or two years ahead
by Lord [INAUDIBLE]----

00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:32.410
and it was the capability
of this new technology

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:36.580
to make the millions
of voiceless people

00:55:36.580 --> 00:55:39.045
up to now become heard.

00:55:39.045 --> 00:55:41.170
So I think we have some
positive things ahead of us

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:42.074
to look forward to.

00:55:42.074 --> 00:55:43.240
Let's have another question.

00:55:46.030 --> 00:55:49.420
AUDIENCE: Mr. Gates,
Alexei [INAUDIBLE],,

00:55:49.420 --> 00:55:51.400
Russian Public
Television-- you were

00:55:51.400 --> 00:55:54.160
pretty critical of people
who are spending hours

00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:56.350
before a television screen.

00:55:56.350 --> 00:55:58.300
But there were some
reports in the press

00:55:58.300 --> 00:56:01.570
that your company,
together with NBC,

00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:03.250
is investing a few
billion dollars

00:56:03.250 --> 00:56:07.320
into a global television
broadcasting system.

00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:08.990
Is it the right
information, first?

00:56:08.990 --> 00:56:12.400
If it is, how will it affect
the nature of the television,

00:56:12.400 --> 00:56:16.660
and what is the difference from
today's satellite broadcasting

00:56:16.660 --> 00:56:17.770
television?

00:56:17.770 --> 00:56:19.120
GATES: No, there's no--

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:22.960
I mean, NBC, together
with Microsoft,

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:26.170
has done a news service
and an internet site.

00:56:26.170 --> 00:56:28.660
But certainly, there
is no investments

00:56:28.660 --> 00:56:30.370
of billions of
dollars or anything

00:56:30.370 --> 00:56:33.940
related to a satellite
infrastructure there.

00:56:33.940 --> 00:56:35.890
It's just one of the
many new services

00:56:35.890 --> 00:56:37.780
that you'll find
on the internet,

00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:42.280
and there's some very, very
healthy competition there.

00:56:42.280 --> 00:56:43.990
DERTOUZOS: Ladies
and gentlemen, we

00:56:43.990 --> 00:56:48.020
have some new high-level
guests with us,

00:56:48.020 --> 00:56:52.810
and we have been asked
to finish this session,

00:56:52.810 --> 00:56:57.820
to close it now, so that we may
hear from our high-level guest.

00:56:57.820 --> 00:57:02.230
So I would like to thank
Bill and the audience

00:57:02.230 --> 00:57:04.420
for a very exciting and
informative session.

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:05.020
Thank you.

00:57:05.020 --> 00:57:08.070
[APPLAUSE]

